Creating a CircleComponent Subclass
To support collision detection of actors, you can create a CircleComponent and a method to
test for intersection between two circle components. You can then add a CircleComponent
to any actor that needs collision.
First, declare CircleComponent as a subclass of Component, as in Listing 3.6. The only mem-
ber data CircleComponent needs is a radius because the center of the circle is simply the
position of the owning actor.
Listing 3.6 CircleComponent Declaration
class CircleComponent : public Component
{
public:
CircleComponent(class Actor* owner);
void SetRadius(float radius) { mRadius = radius; }
float GetRadius() const;
const Vector2& GetCenter() const;
private:
float mRadius;
};
Next, declare a global Intersect function that takes in two circle components by reference and
returns true if the two circles intersect with each other, as in Listing 3.7. Note that the implemen-
tation directly mirrors the equations from the previous section. You first calculate the distance
squared between the two centers and then compare that to the sum of the radii squared.
Listing 3.7 CircleComponent Intersection
bool Intersect(const CircleComponent& a, const CircleComponent& b)
{
// Calculate distance squared
Vector2 diff = a.GetCenter() - b.GetCenter();
float distSq = diff.LengthSq();
// Calculate sum of radii squared
float radiiSq = a.GetRadius() + b.GetRadius();
radiiSq *= radiiSq;
return distSq <= radiiSq;
}
