const GameState* AlphaBetaDecide(const GameState* root, int maxDepth)
{
const GameState* choice = nullptr;
// Alpha starts at negative infinity, beta at positive infinity
float maxValue = -std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();
float beta = std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();
for (const GameState* child : root->GetPossibleMoves())
{
float v = AlphaBetaMin(child, maxDepth - 1, maxValue, beta);
if (v > maxValue)
{
maxValue = v;
choice = child;
}
}
return choice;
}
The implementation of AlphaBetaMax, shown in Listing 4.9, builds on MaxPlayerLimit. If
on any iteration the max value is greater than or equal to beta, it means the score can be no
better than the previous upper bound. This makes it unnecessary to test the remaining siblings,
and so the function returns. Otherwise, the code increases the alpha lower bound if the max
value is greater than alpha.
